Terraform is an open-source Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool developed by HashiCorp. It lets you define, provision, and manage cloud infrastructure using simple, human-readable configuration files written in HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL). Terraform is a tool that helps you automate cloud infrastructure setup. Multi-Cloud Support: 
Concept	Meaning:
Provider	Plugin that connects Terraform to AWS, Azure, GCP, etc.
Plugin means small software to connect terraform with external
Resource	Actual thing you want to create (EC2, S3, RDS, etc.)
Variable	A value you can customize, like instance size
Output	Something Terraform shows after deployment (like EC2 IP)
Module	A module is a reusable and encapsulated unit of Terraform code. Modules allow you to package infrastructure configurations, making it easier to maintain, share, and reuse them across different parts of your infrastructure
Plan	Shows what Terraform is going to do
Apply	Actually creates/updates resources
State File	Keeps track current status of the file and Terraform’s memory of your infrastructure (terraform.tfstate)
Backend	Stores the state file safely (like in S3)
Workspace	Like separate environments: dev, test, prod


You can write all code in a single .tf file like main.tf, but best practice is to split:
•	main.tf → providers + resources
•	variables.tf → inputs
•	outputs.tf → outputs
•	backend.tf (optional) → remote state config	

